<h2>The claim and its dismissal</h2>
<p>The deceased, about sixty, allegedly bought a ticket and boarded an EMU train on 10 December 2022; he fell from the train and died. His wife, son and daughters claimed compensation before the Railway Claims Tribunal, Principal Bench, Delhi. The Tribunal dismissed the claim in July 2024 on the two findings that defeat most railway death claims: that the deceased was not a bona fide passenger, and that his death was not an untoward incident — the Tribunal inferring, from the position of the body, that he had been struck while crossing the tracks. It also leaned on discrepancies between the testimony of the son and the son-in-law, and on the Divisional Railway Manager\'s report.</p>

<h2>The light initial burden on bona fide passenger status</h2>
<p>The appeal turned first on the ticket. Applying <em>Union of India v. Rina Devi</em>, the Court restated the settled allocation of burdens: the claimant\'s initial burden to establish that the deceased was a bona fide passenger is not onerous, and once relevant facts are deposed to, the onus shifts to the Railways. The surrounding circumstances — the journey, the family\'s account of the ticket purchase, the recovery of the body along the route — must be weighed as a whole. Non-recovery of the ticket from a man who died falling from a train cannot be treated as conclusive against his family, and the testimonial discrepancies the Tribunal collected did not justify rejecting passenger status on that ground alone.</p>

<h2>An inference with nothing under it</h2>
<p>The Tribunal\'s alternative theory — death while crossing the tracks — fared worse. The record contained no direct evidence that the deceased had entered the railway line to cross it: the body\'s location and the panchnama were not grounded in any eyewitness account, and the DRM report\'s own version, that he was boarding without a ticket, contradicted the crossing theory while simultaneously conceding that he was boarding. The Court added the point that disposes of many such cases: even an accidental fall while boarding or deboarding is an untoward incident within Section 123(c), attracting the no-fault liability of Section 124-A.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The judgment\'s most useful paragraph for claimants concerns the standard of proof: under a no-fault welfare provision, a claimant is not required to reconstruct every detail of the accident. It is enough that the record, read as a whole, shows on a preponderance of probabilities that the death resulted from an untoward incident. Unwitnessed deaths on the railways are the norm, not the exception, and the statute is not to be administered as if they were murder trials.</p>
</div>

<h2>The result and the practice points</h2>
<p>The appeal was allowed, the Tribunal\'s judgment set aside, and the matter remanded for assessment of compensation in accordance with law, with disbursal directed within two months of the order and the matter listed before the Tribunal in February 2027. For families pursuing claims under Section 124-A, three practice points emerge. Plead and prove the journey\'s circumstances fully — the purchase of the ticket, the purpose of travel, the station sequence — because that is what discharges the light initial burden. Attack inquiry reports that rest on inference rather than eyewitness material; a DRM report is evidence to be weighed, not a verdict. And treat testimonial discrepancies among grieving relatives as explicable human variance — the High Court plainly does. The same framework governs claims arising from Delhi\'s suburban and metro-feeder railway traffic, where falls during boarding remain the commonest fact pattern before the Principal Bench.</p>
